    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Strict control over meiotic division numbers is crucial for gamete generation and conservation of genetic material in sexually reproducing eukaryotes. Although much has been learned about how homologous chromosomes pair, recombine, and segregate in meiosis I, much less is known about the cell cycle regulatory events that lead to two rapid divisions without an intervening S-phase necessary for production of haploid gametes. This project focuses on cell cycle regulation of the two meiotic divisions in the male germ line of Drosophila melanogaster. Previous work in the lab discovered meiotic defects in germ cells lacking function of the COP9 signalosome (csn), which is involved in regulating activities of the Ubiquitin Proteasome System (UPS), which in turn marks proteins for degradation by the 26S proteasome. The failure to undergo meiosis II observed after loss of csn function in spermatocytes is reminiscent of the phenotype caused by over-expression of a previously reported cyclin/CDK inhibitor, roughex (rux), suggesting that csn might negatively regulate rux activity to allow progression of germ cells to meiosis II in the male germ line. Roughex had previously been shown to act as a dosage-dependent regulator of entry into meiosis II via inhibition of cyclin A and B activity. Here, I probed the regulatory relationship between csn and rux using genetic, cell biology, and biochemical approaches. Over-expression of cyclin A and cyclin B1 partially restored chromosome segregation in meiosis II to csn loss of function. Consistent with the hypothesis that csn allows meiosis II by inhibiting rux, I found that knock down of rux in csn mutants showed significant restoration of meiosis II as well. In addition, Western blot analysis showed visibly higher levels of rux protein in csn RNAi testis extracts compared to wild-type controls. My data suggest that csn may be required at a critical time during meiosis II for chromosome segregation by inhibition of rux activity to ensure sufficient cyclin activity.",
